# Second input graph for parseRML (repair audit, 2026-10-31): the zero and
# two-solution cases of the two g.value() reads, and a non-None iterator.
#
# ex:tm3 has NO rr:subjectMap (zero solutions: g.value returns None,
# getSubjectMap(None) returns empty lists -- `.one()` would raise) and its
# logical source carries an rml:iterator, as do both of tm4's, so
# self.iterator ends on the non-None branch.  ex:tm4 has TWO logical sources and TWO subject maps
# (g.value keeps one, `.one()` would raise).
@prefix ex: <http://example.org/> .
@prefix rml: <http://semweb.mmlab.be/ns/rml#> .
@prefix rr: <http://www.w3.org/ns/r2rml#> .
@prefix ql: <http://semweb.mmlab.be/ns/ql#> .

ex:tm3 a rr:TriplesMap ;
    rml:logicalSource ex:src3 .

ex:src3 rml:source "data3.json" ;
    rml:referenceFormulation ql:JSONPath ;
    rml:iterator "$.items[*]" .

ex:tm4 a rr:TriplesMap ;
    rml:logicalSource ex:src4a, ex:src4b ;
    rr:subjectMap ex:sm4a, ex:sm4b .

ex:src4a rml:source "a.csv" ; rml:iterator "$.a" .
ex:src4b rml:source "b.csv" ; rml:iterator "$.b" .
ex:sm4a rr:template "http://example.org/a/{x}" .
ex:sm4b rr:template "http://example.org/b/{y}" .
